    { label: 'Name', value: 'Alfred' },
    { label: 'Tone', value: 'Dry irony' },
    { label: 'Standard', value: 'Impeccable' },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      title: 'What we wanted to build',
      body:
        'We did not want another pretty prompt or a collection of characters performing theatre. We wanted a system that genuinely helps a developer: something that organizes work, detects what matters, keeps continuity between sessions, and pushes toward serious execution.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Why it is called Alfred',
      body:
        'The name comes from Alfred Pennyworth, Batman’s figure who was always where he was needed. He was not the visible protagonist, but he was the one sustaining the operation with judgment, calm, and reliability. Resolute, precise, elegant in execution, and capable of saying things with dry irony when needed without losing professionalism.',
    },
    {
      title: 'What we wanted to bring from that reference',
      body:
        'What mattered was that difficult mix: an assistant with personality but no theatrics; with style but no frivolity; with judgment of its own but in service of the work. Alfred Dev tries to capture exactly that: help you make better decisions, anticipate problems, and keep the bar high without turning the tool into a show.',
    },
    {
      title: 'How that becomes a plugin',
      body:
        'That is why Alfred orchestrates agents, memory, quality gates, traceability, and continuity instead of merely answering prompts. The product ambition is simple to explain and difficult to execute: as a developer, you should feel that you finally have a serious, reliable assistant with real craft.',
